[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"OCP: Strategy Pattern, Plugin Architectures, and Framework Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Open-Closed Principle (OCP) is the 'O' in the SOLID principles of object-oriented design. First coined by Bertrand Meyer, OCP states that software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension, but closed for modification. In this tutorial, we will explore how to achieve OCP using the Strategy Pattern, Plugin Architectures, and Framework Design principles."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Open-Closed Principle"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Being 'closed for modification' means that once a class or module has been developed, tested, and approved, its source code should not be changed to accommodate new requirements. Being 'open for extension' means that the behavior of the module can be extended to meet new needs by adding new code, rather than altering old code."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying the Strategy Pattern for OCP"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Strategy Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that enables selecting an algorithm at runtime. Instead of implementing a single algorithm directly, code receives runtime instructions about which algorithm to use from a family of interchangeable strategies."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"interface PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount: number): void;
}

class CreditCardStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount: number): void {
    console.log("Paid " + amount + " using Credit Card.");
  }
}

class PayPalStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount: number): void {
    console.log("Paid " + amount + " using PayPal.");
  }
}

class ShoppingCart {
  constructor(private paymentStrategy: PaymentStrategy) {}

  checkout(amount: number): void {
    this.paymentStrategy.pay(amount);
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the ShoppingCart class is closed for modification. If a new payment method (e.g., Bitcoin) is introduced, we do not need to alter ShoppingCart. We simply implement a new Bitcoin class that adheres to the PaymentStrategy interface."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Plugin Architectures"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Plugin architectures take OCP a step further by allowing systems to load external extensions dynamically. The core system defines a strict contract (API or Interface), and third-party or modular plugins implement this contract to add features."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Core System: Defines interfaces and manages the lifecycle of plugins."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Plugin Registry: Dynamically discovers and registers plugins."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loose Coupling: Ensures the core does not depend on concrete plugin implementations."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Framework Design Principles"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Framework design relies heavily on OCP and the Hollywood Principle: 'Don't call us, we'll call you.' In a framework, the execution flow is controlled by the framework itself, while developers extend functionality by injecting custom components, interceptors, or event listeners."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Abstract Base Classes and Interfaces to define extension points."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage Dependency Injection (DI) to inject implementations rather than instantiating concrete classes directly."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Design clear extension contracts to prevent fragile base class problems."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining the Strategy Pattern, Plugin Architectures, and robust Framework Design, developers can build scalable, maintainable systems that embrace the Open-Closed Principle. This reduces regression bugs and accelerates feature delivery over the application lifecycle."}]
